**Statement.** For every natural number $\mathrm{hash}$ (any natural number; no bound such as $\mathrm{hash} < 2^{256}$ is assumed) and every 32-bit word $c$ ("nBits"), with top byte $s = \lfloor c/2^{24} \rfloor$ ($0 \le s \le 255$) and low 23 bits $m = c \bmod 2^{23}$, the Boolean value $\mathrm{checkProofOfWork}(\mathrm{hash}, c)$ is **true if and only if** all five of the following hold:

1. the "compact negative" flag $[m \ne 0] \wedge [(c \text{ AND } 2^{23}) \ne 0]$ is false;
2. the "compact overflow" flag $[m \ne 0] \wedge \big([s > 34] \vee ([m > 255] \wedge [s > 33]) \vee ([m > 65535] \wedge [s > 32])\big)$ is false;
3. $0 < T(c)$;
4. $T(c) \le 2^{224} - 1$;
5. $\mathrm{hash} \le T(c)$;

where
$$T(c) = \begin{cases} \lfloor m / 256^{\,3-s} \rfloor & \text{if } s \le 3, \\ m \cdot 256^{\,s-3} & \text{if } s > 3 \end{cases}$$
is the custom "specTarget" value, computed on unbounded natural numbers with **no** reduction modulo $2^{256}$. All inequalities in 3–5 are exactly as written: strict in 3, non-strict in 4 and 5.

**The left-hand side, unfolded.** $\mathrm{checkProofOfWork}(\mathrm{hash}, c)$ is defined through a different quantity, the "setCompact" value
$$S(c) = \begin{cases} \lfloor m / 256^{\,3-s} \rfloor & \text{if } s \le 3, \\ (m \cdot 256^{\,s-3}) \bmod 2^{256} & \text{if } s > 3, \end{cases}$$
as follows: it is false if (negative flag is true) or $S(c) = 0$ or (overflow flag is true) or $2^{224}-1 < S(c)$; otherwise it equals $[\mathrm{hash} \le S(c)]$. So the statement equates a condition phrased with $S$ (reduced modulo $2^{256}$) on the left with a condition phrased with $T$ (unreduced) on the right.

**Binders and hypotheses.** Two universally quantified variables, $\mathrm{hash}$ and $c$; no hypotheses. The claim is a biconditional between "the Boolean equals true" and a five-fold conjunction of propositions. Both directions are asserted: whenever the check returns true, conditions 1–5 hold; and whenever 1–5 hold, the check returns true.

**Edge cases visible in the statement.** If $m = 0$ then both flags are false, $T(c) = 0$, condition 3 fails, and the right side is false. If $s \le 3$, both flags are false and $T(c) = S(c) \le m < 2^{23}$. The right side permits $T(c) = 2^{224}-1$ exactly (non-strict cap) and $\mathrm{hash} = T(c)$ exactly. Because $\mathrm{hash}$ is unbounded, the statement covers $\mathrm{hash} \ge 2^{256}$, where condition 5 forces the right side false since $T(c) \le 2^{224}-1$ under condition 4.

**Read-back of `BitcoinUtxo.tx_conservation`.**

Let $T$ be any type with decidable equality. A *UTXO set* $u$ over $T$ is a finite ordered list of pairs (outpoint, coin): an outpoint is $\langle \mathrm{txid}, n\rangle$ with $\mathrm{txid} \in T$ and $n \in \mathbb{N}$; a coin $c$ has an output $c.\mathrm{out}$ (integer $c.\mathrm{out}.\mathrm{value}$, Boolean $c.\mathrm{out}.\mathrm{unspendable}$), a height $c.\mathrm{height} \in \mathbb{N}$ and a Boolean $c.\mathrm{isCoinbase}$. $u[p]$ is the coin of the first entry of $u$ with outpoint $p$, or "none". $\mathrm{tv}(u)$ is the integer sum of $c.\mathrm{out}.\mathrm{value}$ over all entries of $u$. $\mathrm{keysNodup}(u)$ says the outpoints of $u$ are pairwise distinct. A *transaction* $\mathrm{tx}$ has fields $\mathrm{txid} \in T$, $\mathrm{vin}$ (list of outpoints), $\mathrm{vout}$ (list of outputs $o$ with integer $o.\mathrm{value}$ and Boolean $o.\mathrm{unspendable}$) and Boolean $\mathrm{isCoinbase}$. $\mathrm{MAX\_MONEY} = 2.1 \times 10^{15}$; $\mathrm{moneyRange}(v) \iff 0 \le v \le \mathrm{MAX\_MONEY}$.

*Spending and adding.* $\mathrm{spendCoin}(u, p)$ deletes every entry of outpoint $p$; $\mathrm{spendInputs}(u, [p_1,\dots,p_m])$ applies this successively for $p_1, \dots, p_m$. $\mathrm{addCoin}(u, p, c)$ is $u$ unchanged if $c.\mathrm{out}.\mathrm{unspendable}$ is true, and otherwise $(p, c)$ prepended to $\mathrm{spendCoin}(u, p)$. Then
$$\mathrm{updateCoins}(u, \mathrm{tx}, h) = \text{the result of adding, for } j = 0, 1, \dots, |\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vout}| - 1 \text{ in order, the coin } \langle o_j, h, \mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{isCoinbase}\rangle \text{ at outpoint } \langle \mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{txid}, j\rangle \text{ to } u_0,$$
where $o_j$ is the $j$-th output of $\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vout}$, and $u_0 = u$ if $\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{isCoinbase}$ is true, $u_0 = \mathrm{spendInputs}(u, \mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vin})$ otherwise. (Each addition is applied to the result of the previous one; an unspendable output is skipped but its index is still consumed.)

*The input check.* $\mathrm{checkTxInputs}(u, \mathrm{tx}, h)$ returns an optional integer: none if $\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{isCoinbase}$ is false and some input of $\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vin}$ has no entry in $u$; otherwise it runs through $\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vin} = [p_1, \dots, p_m]$ with running total $a_0 = 0$, returning none if some $u[p_i]$ is none, or if $u[p_i] = $ some $c_i$ with $c_i.\mathrm{isCoinbase}$ true and $h \mathbin{\dot-} c_i.\mathrm{height} < 100$ (truncated natural subtraction, so in particular whenever $c_i.\mathrm{height} \ge h$), or if $\mathrm{moneyRange}(c_i.\mathrm{out}.\mathrm{value})$ or $\mathrm{moneyRange}(a_i)$ fails where $a_i = a_{i-1} + c_i.\mathrm{out}.\mathrm{value}$; otherwise, with $v_{\mathrm{in}} = a_m$ and $v_{\mathrm{out}} = \sum_{o \in \mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vout}} o.\mathrm{value}$, it returns none if $v_{\mathrm{in}} < v_{\mathrm{out}}$ or if $\mathrm{moneyRange}(v_{\mathrm{in}} - v_{\mathrm{out}})$ fails, and otherwise some $(v_{\mathrm{in}} - v_{\mathrm{out}})$.

The theorem states: for every type $T$ with decidable equality, every UTXO set $u$ over $T$, every transaction $\mathrm{tx}$ over $T$, every height $h \in \mathbb{N}$ and every integer $\mathrm{fee}$, **if**

- $\mathrm{keysNodup}(u)$: the outpoints of $u$ are pairwise distinct;
- the list $\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vin}$ has no duplicate outpoints;
- $\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{isCoinbase} = \mathrm{false}$;
- $\mathrm{checkTxInputs}(u, \mathrm{tx}, h) = \text{some } \mathrm{fee}$ (with the same $h$ used as the spend height);
- (freshness) for every natural number $j$ with $j < |\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vout}|$, $u[\langle \mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{txid}, j\rangle] = \text{none}$ — i.e. in the *original* set $u$ (before any inputs are spent) there is no entry at any of the outpoints $\langle \mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{txid}, 0\rangle, \dots, \langle \mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{txid}, |\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vout}|-1\rangle$; nothing is said about indices $\ge |\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vout}|$, and the condition is vacuous if $\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vout}$ is empty;
- for every output $o$ occurring in $\mathrm{tx}.\mathrm{vout}$, $0 \le o.\mathrm{value}$;

**then**
$$\mathrm{tv}\big(\mathrm{updateCoins}(u, \mathrm{tx}, h)\big) \le \mathrm{tv}(u) - \mathrm{fee},$$
a non-strict inequality of integers (not an equality).

The same $h$ serves both as the spend height in the input check and as the creation height of the new coins. There is no hypothesis that $\mathrm{tx}$ passes `checkTransaction` (its $\mathrm{vin}$ or $\mathrm{vout}$ may be empty, and the output values are only required to be $\ge 0$, not $\le \mathrm{MAX\_MONEY}$). The hypotheses are jointly satisfiable only when every input of $\mathrm{tx}$ is present in $u$ with a value in $[0, \mathrm{MAX\_MONEY}]$, so a transaction with no inputs satisfies the fourth hypothesis only if $\sum_{o} o.\mathrm{value} \le 0$.

